BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:heart2world-book-prize-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260805
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:Deadline: Heart2World Publishing: Heart2World Book Prize 2026 (Inau
 gural\, Poetry)
DESCRIPTION:The inaugural Heart2World Book Prize\, from Heart2World Publish
 ing\, is a poetry prize for a full-length collection by a Nigerian writer.
  AWARD: NGN 250\,000 cash prize\, a full publishing and marketing package 
 (valued at approximately NGN 5 million)\, and 100 percent net royalties on
  the published collection. ELIGIBILITY: Nigerian citizens currently residi
 ng in Nigeria\; submit an unpublished poetry collection of 30 to 35 poems 
 written in English. There is no entry or reading fee (verified on the fund
 er page). DEADLINE: 5 August 2026. Apply via the prize page.\n\nOrganisati
 on: Heart2World Publishing\nLocation: Nigeria\; open to Nigerian writers c
 urrently residing in Nigeria.\nAward: NGN 250\,000 cash prize\, a full pub
 lishing and marketing package (valued at approximately NGN 5 million)\, an
 d 100 percent net royalties on the published collection. No entry or readi
 ng fee.\nDuration: One-off prize\; publication to follow\n\nMore: https://
 heart2worldpublishing.org/prize/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://heart2worldpublishing.org/prize/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Nigeria\; open to Nigerian writers currently residing in Nigeria.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:berggruen-prize-essay-competition-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260818
SUMMARY:Deadline: Berggruen Prize Essay Competition 2026: A New Axial Age?
DESCRIPTION:Annual essay competition from the Berggruen Institute\, a compa
 nion to the Berggruen Prize for Philosophy & Culture\, seeking new thinkin
 g and cross-cultural perspectives on fundamental philosophical questions o
 f significance for contemporary life and the future. It looks for essays i
 n the tradition of Rousseau\, Montaigne\, and Emerson (models cited includ
 e Frankfurt\, Nagel\, Sontag\, Fukuyama\, and recent Noema pieces)\, not p
 eer-reviewed academic work: novel ideas\, clearly argued in compelling way
 s for intellectually serious readers. THEME for 2026: 'A New Axial Age?' R
 eferencing Karl Jaspers's Axial Age (c. 800-200 BCE)\, the prompt asks whe
 ther civilisation is again at a 'hinge of history' undergoing a pivotal tr
 ansformation of consciousness driven by rapid technological change and its
  social\, economic\, and political consequences. Suggested (non-exhaustive
 ) angles: the foundational ideas and institutions underpinning present and
  future societies\; the philosophical implications of the current era\; th
 e future of human evolution and life amid the ecological crisis\; shared c
 onceptual foundations for a common future\; global transformation driven b
 y technology and the rise of AI\; the end of the global order and its rami
 fications\; and the future of life amid biotechnology and synthetic life. 
 Entrants may choose their own topic\; for 2026 creative fiction is accepte
 d. ELIGIBILITY: open to the public\; not for academic papers and not gover
 ned by academic disciplinary standards or affiliations. Length up to 10\,0
 00 words in English or 15\,000 characters in Chinese (excluding diagrams\,
  tables\, endnotes\, bibliography\, or authorship declaration). One submis
 sion per year per entrant\, in either English or Chinese\; co-authorship a
 llowed. Submissions must be original and previously unpublished. AI USAGE:
  generative AI must not be used to create content or substitute original t
 hought\, and entrants must engage primary sources rather than AI summaries
 \; AI is permitted only for proofreading/grammar\, as quoted supporting ma
 terial when AI itself is the subject of the research\, or for translating 
 an essay into English or Chinese\, and all AI use (including AI-assisted t
 ranslation) must be disclosed on the front page or the entry is disqualifi
 ed. JUDGING: originality\, clarity\, and style. Essays are due by 23:59 PT
  on 17 August 2026\; PDF only. Chinese-language submissions use a separate
  page. Inquiries to essay@berggruen.org.\n\nOrganisation: Berggruen Instit
 ute\nLocation: Remote/online\; open to the public worldwide (English and C
 hinese submissions)\nAward: USD 50\,000 for the winning English essay and 
 USD 50\,000 for the winning Chinese essay. If there is more than one winne
 r in a language category\, the prize is split evenly. Winning essays are p
 ublished through BI Press or Noema. No entry fee.\nDuration: One-off compe
 tition\; submissions open 17 March 2026 and close 17 August 2026\n\nMore: 
 https://berggruen.org/eu/essay-competition-open?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://berggruen.org/eu/essay-competition-open?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote/online\; open to the public worldwide (English and Chinese 
 submissions)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:richell-prize-emerging-writers-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260713
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260714
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Richell Prize for Emerging Writers 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Richell Prize for Emerging Writers\, presented by the Emerg
 ing Writers' Festival and Hachette Australia\, is an annual prize for unpu
 blished writers of adult fiction and adult narrative non-fiction. The winn
 er receives AUD $10\,000 and a 12-month mentorship with a Hachette Austral
 ia publisher\, who will work with the writer to develop their manuscript\,
  with first option to consider the finished work for publication. Long- an
 d short-listed writers are also eligible to apply for a Casa Morphosi Resi
 dency in Abruzzo\, Italy. The prize was established in memory of Hachette 
 Australia CEO Matt Richell (d. 2014). ELIGIBILITY: must currently reside i
 n Australia (Australian citizens\, permanent residents\, and long-term vis
 a holders residing in Australia\; those residing overseas are not eligible
 )\; unpublished writers only -- no previous publication by a publishing ho
 use (including small press or independent publishers in any genre)\; publi
 cation in anthologies\, literary journals\, scholarly works\, writing PhD 
 projects\, or self-publishing does not disqualify. ELIGIBLE GENRES: adult 
 fiction (including short story collections) and adult narrative non-fictio
 n (including memoir and essay collections). Poetry\, scripts\, plays\, pic
 ture books\, practical non-fiction\, and works for children or young adult
 s are not eligible. SUBMISSION FORMAT: single PDF comprising the first thr
 ee chapters (maximum 20\,000 words)\, a one-page synopsis\, and a chapter 
 breakdown of up to two A4 pages\; plus a statement (up to 750 words) on ho
 w winning the prize will further your writing career\, entered in the onli
 ne form. Manuscript must be complete or intended to be completed\; does no
 t need to be finished at time of submission. A4\, double-spaced\, 12pt Rom
 an font (e.g. Garamond or Times New Roman)\, 3cm margins\, page numbers. T
 IMELINE: prize opened 23 April 2026\; closes 13 July 2026 at 11:59pm AEST\
 ; longlist announced 11 September 2026\; shortlist 16 October 2026\; winne
 r announced 6 November 2026. Administered with support from Simpsons Solic
 itors. Apply at https://emergingwritersfestival.org.au/richell-prize-2026/
 .\n\nOrganisation: Emerging Writers' Festival and Hachette Australia\nLoca
 tion: Australia\; must currently reside in Australia.\nAward: AUD $10\,000
  prize money (donated by Hachette Australia)\; 12-month mentorship with a 
 Hachette Australia publisher\; first option to consider finished work for 
 publication. Long- and short-listed writers also eligible to apply for a C
 asa Morphosi Residency in Abruzzo\, Italy. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 12-month mentorship.\n\nMore: https://emergingwritersfestival.org.au/riche
 ll-prize-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://emergingwritersfestival.org.au/richell-prize-2026/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Australia\; must currently reside in Australia.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jamverse-jam-protocolized-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Jamverse Jam (Protocolized worldbuilding contest)
DESCRIPTION:Protocolized's fourth contest invites artists and writers (stor
 ytellers\, poets\, comic artists) to build\, extend\, and connect the Jamv
 erse\, a shared near-future science fiction universe of four intersecting 
 story cycles (The Stockton Chronicles\, Legends and Ledgers\, UET-Trainver
 se\, and Zoothesia). ELIGIBILITY: open to anyone\; you may submit up to th
 ree in-universe worldbuilding artifacts\, and the same creator can win mul
 tiple prizes. REQUIREMENTS: each artifact must be under 1\,000 words (200-
 700 is the sweet spot) or a visual equivalent (comic panel\, poster\, adve
 rtisement\, etc.) and must include at least one element drawn from one of 
 the four sub-world 'toy boxes'\, a surprising 'traffic jam' (an emergent\,
  ah-ha consequence of a technology or protocol)\, and a relation to the th
 eme of Stigmergy (indirect coordination through modifying a shared environ
 ment). Artifact types can include comic panels\, job postings\, poems\, fo
 rum threads\, historic documents\, or encyclopedia entries. APPLY: submit 
 each artifact as a public Google Doc via the contest form\, including the 
 artifact\, a one-paragraph explanation of the worlds/toy-box components us
 ed and how the piece relates to stigmergy and explores a traffic jam\, and
 \, if AI was used\, documentation of your AI/LLM protocol. PRIZES: one USD
  1\,000 grand prize and at least 10 USD 200 prizes\, all published on the 
 Jamverse website. DEADLINE: 31 July 2026. Contest rules\, the current Jamv
 erse stories\, and starter materials are on the Jamverse website.\n\nOrgan
 isation: Protocolized (Summer of Protocols)\nLocation: Online / Worldwide\
 nAward: One USD 1\,000 grand prize plus at least 10 additional USD 200 pri
 zes\; winning artifacts are published on the Jamverse website. No entry fe
 e.\nDuration: One-off contest\; submit up to three artifacts by 31 July 20
 26\n\nMore: https://protocolized.summerofprotocols.com/p/jamverse-jam?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://protocolized.summerofprotocols.com/p/jamverse-jam?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online / Worldwide
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:woodward-newman-award-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151548Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Woodward/Newman Award 2027-28 (Constellation Stage & Scre
 en)
DESCRIPTION:The Woodward/Newman Award\, started with the support of Joanne 
 Woodward\, Newman's Own Foundation and the Newman family\, is awarded annu
 ally by Constellation Stage & Screen to the best unpublished new play. The
  winner receives a USD 3\,000 cash prize (in lieu of royalties) and a full
  production in the Mainstage season\, with housing and transportation prov
 ided. The contest format and submission fees have been eliminated: submiss
 ions are reviewed on an ongoing basis and are also considered for all seve
 n production slots in the season\, including TYA (theatre for young audien
 ces) shows. Full-length plays should run 75 to 135 minutes\; TYA shows ove
 r 40 minutes. Plays must be unpublished at submission (independently publi
 shed is acceptable)\; limit of two submissions per playwright per year\; m
 usicals may include demos. Plays submitted by midnight 31 August 2026 are 
 considered for the 2027-28 season\; later submissions roll to 2028-29. All
  submissions are kept on file for two years.\n\nOrganisation: Constellatio
 n Stage & Screen\nLocation: Bloomington\, Indiana\, USA. Open submissions 
 of unpublished plays\; production takes place in Bloomington.\nAward: USD 
 3\,000 cash prize (in lieu of royalties) plus a full production in Constel
 lation's Mainstage season\; housing and transportation provided. No submis
 sion fee.\nDuration: Winner announced by 15 May 2027\; production in the 2
 027-28 season. Submissions stay on file and under consideration for two ye
 ars.\n\nMore: https://seeconstellation.org/get-involved/submit-play/?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://seeconstellation.org/get-involved/submit-play/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Bloomington\, Indiana\, USA. Open submissions of unpublished plays
 \; production takes place in Bloomington.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
